#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/Working-with-interfaces/Single-Package-Define-And-Use-Interface.rst:51
msgid "Before creating a custom interface, do the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/Working-with-interfaces/Single-Package-Define-And-Use-Interface.rst:53
msgid "Check whether a suitable standard message already exists."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/Working-with-interfaces/Single-Package-Define-And-Use-Interface.rst:54
msgid ""
"If no single standard message fits your use case, consider creating a new "
"message composed of standard messages. See standard messages here: https://"
"github.com/ros2/common_interfaces."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/Working-with-interfaces/Single-Package-Define-And-Use-Interface.rst:57
msgid "Creating a completely custom message should be the last resort."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/Working-with-interfaces/Single-Package-Define-And-Use-Interface.rst:59
msgid ""
"Creating custom interfaces involves preparing a package, specifying "
"interface definitions, and registering the interfaces in ``package.xml`` and "
"``CMakeLists.txt``. Using custom interfaces involves configuring a node to "
"include the interfaces in its source, and configuring the node to build with "
"the interfaces in ``CMakeLists.txt``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/interfaces/Working-with-interfaces/Single-Package-Define-And-Use-Interface.rst:64
msgid ""
"The best practice is to declare interfaces in dedicated interface packages, "
"but sometimes it may be more convenient for you to declare, create and use "
"an interface all in one package. Using a dedicated interface package is "
"preferred because it allows multiple packages to share message definitions "
"without sharing any other code contained in the package."
msgstr ""

